const title = "Whirlpool Dishwasher Not Cleaning? Causes & Fix — Same Day";
const description = `Whirlpool dishwasher leaving food on dishes? Same-day LA. The OWI sensor, filter vs hard-food-disposer models, blocked spray arm. Also KitchenAid/Maytag. $89 dx waived.`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "My Whirlpool cleans worse than it used to but throws no error code. What's the Whirlpool-specific cause?",
    a: "On Whirlpool-family dishwashers, one culprit that's specific to the brand is the OWI — the Optical Water Indicator, a small sensor that reads how dirty the wash water is and decides how long and hard to run. When the OWI's window gets fouled with film or hard-water scale, it can misread dirty water as clean and cut the wash cycle short, so dishes come out under-washed with no error at all. Cleaning or replacing the OWI often restores full cleaning. Beyond that, check the filter and spray arms first (below). $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair."
  },
  {
    q: "Does my Whirlpool have a filter I need to clean, or a food grinder?",
    a: "Depends on the model, and it changes what you do. Older Whirlpool dishwashers used a hard-food disposer — a grinder that chops food so there's no manual filter to clean. Newer models switched to a manual filter (a cylinder at the bottom of the tub) that DOES need regular cleaning; a clogged filter is a top cause of poor cleaning and redeposited grit on those. Look at the tub bottom: a twist-out filter cylinder means clean it monthly; a fixed grate with no removable filter means it's a disposer model. If it's a filter model and you've never cleaned it, start there."
  },
  {
    q: "The bottom rack cleans fine but the top rack doesn't (or vice versa).",
    a: "That points at the spray arms or the diverter. Each rack has its own spray arm; if one arm's holes are clogged with hard-water scale or debris, or the arm can't spin because a tall item is blocking it, that rack won't get cleaned. Whirlpool also uses a diverter that directs water between the upper and lower arms — if the diverter is failing (it can throw an F9 code), one level gets starved of water. Pull each spray arm, check that it spins freely and the holes are clear, and make sure nothing tall is stopping its rotation."
  },
  {
    q: "Could it be the wash pump or chopper?",
    a: "Yes, on a Whirlpool that's cleaning weakly across both racks with clean filter and spray arms. The wash (circulation) pump provides the spray pressure; a worn pump or a chopper area jammed with debris (a chunk of glass, a fruit pit) drops pressure so nothing sprays hard enough to clean. This is a technician diagnosis — we check pressure and inspect the chopper and pump. Circulation-pump work runs $260 to $520. It's less common than the OWI, filter, and spray-arm causes, so we rule those out first."
  },
  {
    q: "Is a white film on everything a cleaning failure?",
    a: "Usually not — that's hard-water mineral film, which is common in Southern California and separate from the dishwasher not cleaning. Rinse aid, hotter water, and (on units with a softener) dishwasher salt handle it. The universal cleaning basics — water temperature, detergent freshness, rinse aid, loading — apply to every brand, so we keep the full rundown on our general dishwasher not cleaning page. This Whirlpool page focuses on what's distinct to Whirlpool: the OWI sensor, the filter-vs-disposer design, and the spray/diverter path."
  },
  {
    q: "Does this apply to KitchenAid and Maytag too?",
    a: "Largely yes — KitchenAid, Maytag, and JennAir dishwashers share the Whirlpool platform, including the OWI sensor design, spray-arm and diverter layout, and the filter-vs-disposer split by era. The specific parts come from the same catalog families. So the Whirlpool-specific causes here carry over to those brands; we treat them as one platform for diagnosis and parts."
  },
  {
    q: "What quick things should I try before booking a visit?",
    a: "Clean the filter (if it's a filter model) — twist out the cylinder at the tub bottom and rinse it. Pull the spray arms and clear the holes with a toothpick, and confirm each spins freely with nothing blocking it. Run the hot tap at the sink before starting so the fill water is hot (Whirlpool cleaning suffers on cold fill). Use fresh detergent (old detergent clumps and under-performs) and keep rinse aid full. Run a cleaning cycle with a dishwasher cleaner to cut grease and scale. If cleaning is still poor after all that, the OWI sensor, spray path, or wash pump is the likely culprit."
  },
  {
    q: "When is Whirlpool 'not cleaning' worth a repair versus replacement?",
    a: "An OWI sensor, a spray arm, or a filter fix is inexpensive and always worth it. A wash-pump or diverter repair on a newer machine is worth it too. The place to weigh replacement is a wash-pump or control failure on an older unit that's also showing other faults, where the combined cost approaches a new mid-range dishwasher. We give you that math at the door. $89 diagnostic, waived if you go ahead with the repair."
  },
  {
    q: "What's your warranty?",
    a: "90 days on SDAR labor and parts for the work we perform — separate from any Whirlpool factory warranty. Same component fails inside 90 days, we replace it free. BHGS #A49573, BBB Accredited Business, EPA 608 Universal certified #1346255700410. $89 residential diagnostic, waived when you approve the repair."
  }
];

