<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Answer first</p>
    <h2>Two things are specifically Whirlpool here: the OWI sensor, and whether your model has a filter or a grinder.</h2>

    <p><strong>The universal cleaning basics — water temperature, detergent, rinse aid, loading, hard water — apply to every dishwasher, and we keep that full rundown on our <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/not-cleaning/">general dishwasher not cleaning</a> page. What's distinct to Whirlpool: the <span class="hl">OWI (Optical Water Indicator)</span> sensor, which reads how dirty the water is and can cut a cycle short when it's fouled, so dishes come out under-washed with no error code; and the <span class="hl">filter-vs-disposer design split</span> — older models grind food, newer ones have a manual filter you must clean. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair. BHGS #A49573, EPA 608 Universal certified (#1346255700410), BBB Accredited.</strong></p>

    <p>So on a Whirlpool that's cleaning poorly, our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair work the brand-specific suspects first: is it a filter model that's never been cleaned, is the OWI window fouled, are the spray arms spinning and clear, and is the diverter feeding both racks. Then, if needed, the wash pump. Below we walk each.</p>

    <p>Everything here applies to <strong>KitchenAid and Maytag</strong> (same platform). Parent pillar: <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/">dishwasher repair</a>. Codes: <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/whirlpool-error-codes/">Whirlpool dishwasher error codes</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Cause 1 · Whirlpool-specific</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The OWI sensor — a clean-looking cycle that quits early.</h2>
    <p>Whirlpool-family dishwashers use an Optical Water Indicator (OWI), a small optical sensor that measures how dirty the wash water is and tells the control how long and how hard to run. It's a smart idea — a lightly soiled load gets a shorter cycle. But when the OWI's little window fogs over with grease film or hard-water scale (common in our water), it can misread dirty water as clean and end the wash early. The dishes come out under-cleaned, and there's usually no error code because nothing "failed" — the sensor just lied to the control.</p>
    <p>The fix is often cleaning the OWI window, or replacing the sensor if it's degraded. It can also surface as an F3 code (the thermistor/OWI family). This is the cause most people never suspect, which is exactly why we check it early on a Whirlpool.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Cause 2 · Whirlpool-specific</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Filter model or hard-food disposer? It changes your maintenance.</h2>
    <p>Older Whirlpool dishwashers used a hard-food disposer — a grinder that chops food waste, so there's no manual filter to clean. Newer models moved to a manual filter: a twist-out cylinder at the bottom of the tub that needs cleaning every few weeks. If you have a filter model and have never cleaned it, a clogged filter is a leading cause of poor cleaning and gritty redeposit — food that got washed off simply gets recirculated back onto the dishes.</p>
    <p>Check the tub floor: a removable filter cylinder means clean it monthly; a fixed grate with no removable element means it's a disposer model (where a jammed chopper, not a dirty filter, would be the issue). Knowing which you have tells you whether "clean the filter" is even your fix.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Cause 3</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Spray arms and the diverter — when one rack cleans and the other doesn't.</h2>
    <p>Each rack has its own spray arm. If an arm's holes clog with scale or debris, or a tall item blocks the arm from spinning, that rack won't get cleaned — classic "top rack fine, bottom rack dirty" (or the reverse). Pull each arm, clear the holes with a toothpick, and confirm it spins freely. Whirlpool also uses a diverter that steers water between the upper and lower arms; a failing diverter (which can throw an F9 code) starves one level of water. If one rack is consistently poor and the arm is clear and spinning, the diverter is the next suspect.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Cause 4</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Wash pump or chopper — weak spray across both racks.</h2>
    <p>If cleaning is weak everywhere, and the filter, OWI, and spray arms all check out, the wash (circulation) pump is the suspect — it provides the spray pressure. A worn pump, or a chopper area jammed with a shard of glass or a fruit pit, drops pressure so nothing sprays hard enough to clean. This is a technician diagnosis: we check spray pressure and inspect the pump and chopper. Circulation-pump work runs $260 to $520. It's less common than the first three causes, so we confirm those are clear before opening the pump.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">The universal basics</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Don't skip these — they fix most cleaning complaints on any brand.</h2>
    <p>Before it's a Whirlpool-specific part, run the basics that apply to every dishwasher: fill water should be hot (run the sink's hot tap first — cold water under-cleans), detergent should be fresh (old detergent clumps and underperforms), rinse aid full, and the load spaced so water reaches every surface. White film is usually hard-water minerals, not a cleaning failure — rinse aid and a cleaning cycle handle it. Our <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/not-cleaning/">general dishwasher not cleaning</a> page covers all of that in depth for every brand; this page is the Whirlpool layer on top.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">DIY checklist</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before you book a visit.</h2>
    <ol class="num-list">
      <li><strong>Clean the filter</strong> (if it's a filter model) — twist out the cylinder, rinse, reinstall.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the OWI window</strong> area for film/scale (it lives near the sump); a cleaning cycle helps.</li>
      <li><strong>Pull the spray arms,</strong> clear the holes, confirm each spins freely with nothing blocking it.</li>
      <li><strong>Run the hot tap</strong> at the sink first so fill water is hot.</li>
      <li><strong>Fresh detergent + full rinse aid;</strong> run a dishwasher-cleaner cycle to cut grease and scale.</li>
      <li><strong>Space the load</strong> so nothing shadows another item from the spray.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Still poor after all that? The OWI sensor, diverter, or wash pump is the likely culprit — that's a service call.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Honest opinion</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Most Whirlpool cleaning fixes are cheap. One scenario isn't.</h2>
    <p>An OWI sensor, a spray arm, or a filter clean is inexpensive and always worth doing. A diverter or wash-pump repair on a newer machine is worth it too. The one place we'll tell you to weigh a new machine is a wash-pump or control failure on an older Whirlpool that's already showing other faults, where the combined cost gets close to a new mid-range dishwasher. We give you that math honestly at the door. $89 diagnostic, waived if you approve the repair.</p>
  </div>
</section>

